<exercisegroup cols="2">
<title>Computing determinants</title>

<introduction><p> Compute the determinant of each matrix. </p></introduction>

<exercise>
	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle \begin{bmatrix} -9 \end{bmatrix} </m></p></statement>
	<answer><p><m>\displaystyle \det \begin{bmatrix} -9 \end{bmatrix} = -9 </m></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>
	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle \begin{bmatrix} 0 \end{bmatrix} </m></p></statement>
	<answer><p><m>\displaystyle \det \begin{bmatrix} 0 \end{bmatrix} = 0 </m></p></answer>
</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			-5 \amp -4 \\
			 1 \amp  2
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />),
		we have
		<me>
			\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
				-5 \amp -4 \\
				 1 \amp  2
			\end{avmatrix}
			= (-5) \cdot 2 - (-4) \cdot 1
			= -6
		</me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			2 \amp 5 \\
			1 \amp 2
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />),
		we have
		<me>
			\begin{vmatrix}
				2 \amp 5 \\
				1 \amp 2
			\end{vmatrix}
			= 2 \cdot 2 - 5 \cdot 1
			= -1
		</me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
			-5 \amp 6 \\
			 4 \amp 5
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />),
		we have
		<me>
			\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
				-5 \amp 6 \\
				 4 \amp 5
			\end{avmatrix}
			= (-5) \cdot 5 - 6 \cdot 4
			= -49
		</me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>
<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			1 \amp 6 \\
			6 \amp 0
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />),
		we have
		<me>
			\begin{vmatrix}
				1 \amp 6 \\
				6 \amp 0
			\end{vmatrix}
			= 1 \cdot 0 - 6 \cdot 6
			= -36
		</me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 1 \amp -1 \amp  4 \\
			-2 \amp  5 \amp -1 \\
			 4 \amp -1 \amp -3
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Expanding along the first row,
		we have
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{lll}
					\phantom{-}{\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^+ \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{-1}}^- \amp \phantom{-}{\color{red}\mathbf{4}}^+ \\
					               -2                    \amp        \phantom{-}5        \amp                 -1                   \\
					           \phantom{-}4              \amp            -1              \amp                 -3
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				1 \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					 5 \amp -1 \\
					-1 \amp -3
				\end{avmatrix}
				- (-1) \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp -1 \\
					 4 \amp -3
				\end{avmatrix}
				+ 4 \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp  5 \\
					 4 \amp -1
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\bbrac{5 \cdot (-3) - (-1) \cdot (-1)}
				+ \bbrac{(-2) \cdot (-3) - (-1) \cdot 4}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp \phantom{=} \quad
				+ 4 \bbrac{(-2) \cdot (-1) - 5 \cdot 4}
			</mrow>

			<mrow> \amp = -78 </mrow>

		</md>.
		To compute the minor determinants in this cofactor expansion we have used the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix.
		(See <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />.)
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
			 1 \amp  3 \amp   7 \\
			 1 \amp  1 \amp   0 \\
			-1 \amp -4 \amp -11
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Expand along the second row so that there are only two cofactors to compute:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{lll}
					           \phantom{-}1              \amp            \phantom{-}3              \amp           \phantom{-1}7              \\
					\phantom{-}{\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^- \amp \phantom{-}{\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^+ \amp \phantom{-}{\color{red}\mathbf{0}}^- \\
					                -1                   \amp                 -4                   \amp                -11
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				-1 \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					 3 \amp   7 \\
					-4 \amp -11
				\end{avmatrix}
				+ 1 \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					 1 \amp   7 \\
					-1 \amp -11
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				- \bbrac{3 \cdot (-11) - 7 \cdot (-4)}
				+ \bbrac{1 \cdot (-11) - 7 \cdot (-1)}
			</mrow>

			<mrow> \amp = 1 </mrow>

		</md>.
		To compute the minor determinants in this cofactor expansion we have used the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the determinant of a <m>2 \times 2</m> matrix.
		(See <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />.)
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
			-2 \amp  0 \amp  3 \amp  7 \\
			-3 \amp -1 \amp  2 \amp -2 \\
			 3 \amp  1 \amp -2 \amp  3 \\
			 1 \amp  0 \amp -1 \amp -3
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Choose the second column for a cofactor expansion since it will involve the fewest minor determinant calculations:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{rrrr}
					-2 \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{ 0}}^- \amp  3 \amp  7 \\
					-3 \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{-1}}^+ \amp  2 \amp -2 \\
					 3 \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{ 1}}^- \amp -2 \amp  3 \\
					 1 \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{ 0}}^+ \amp -1 \amp -3
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				(-1) \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rrr}
					-2 \amp  3 \amp  7 \\
					 3 \amp -2 \amp  3 \\
					 1 \amp -1 \amp -3
				\end{avmatrix}
				- 1 \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rrr}
					-2 \amp  3 \amp  7 \\
					-3 \amp  2 \amp -2 \\
					 1 \amp -1 \amp -3
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>
		Let's compute these two <m>3 \times 3</m> minor determinants separately;
		call the first matrix <m>A_1</m> and the second <m>A_2</m>.
		Neither has any zero entries,
		but the third row in each contains a couple of ones,
		so we'll choose to expand along that row in each.
		In both expansions,
		we will use the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the resulting <m>2 \times 2</m> minor determinants
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />).
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A_1 \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{lll}
					           -2             \amp        \phantom{-}3        \amp       \phantom{-}7         \\
					       \phantom{-}3       \amp            -2              \amp       \phantom{-}3         \\
					{\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^+ \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{-1}}^- \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{-3}}^+
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				1 \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					 3 \amp 7 \\
					-2 \amp 3 \\
				\end{avmatrix}
				- (-1) \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp 7 \\
					 3 \amp 3 \\
				\end{avmatrix}
				+ (-3) \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp  3 \\
					 3 \amp -2
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\bbrac{3 \cdot 3 - 7 \cdot (-2)}
				+ \bbrac{(-2) \cdot 3 - 7 \cdot 3}
				-  3 \bbrac{(-2) \cdot (-2) - 3 \cdot 3}
			</mrow>

			<mrow> \amp = 11 </mrow>

		</md>
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A_2 \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{lll}
					                      -2             \amp        \phantom{-}3        \amp        \phantom{-}7        \\
					                      -3             \amp        \phantom{-}2        \amp            -2              \\
					\phantom{-}{\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^+ \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{-1}}^- \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{-3}}^+
				\end{avmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				1 \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					3 \amp  7 \\
					2 \amp -2 \\
				\end{avmatrix}
				- (-1) \cdot
				\begin{avmatrix}{rr}
					-2 \amp  7 \\
					-3 \amp -2 \\
				\end{avmatrix}
				+ (-3) \cdot
				\begin{vmatrix}
					-2 \amp 3 \\
					-3 \amp 2
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				\bbrac{3 \cdot (-2) - 7 \cdot 2}
				+ \bbrac{(-2) \cdot (-2) - 7 \cdot (-3)}
				- 3 \bbrac{(-2) \cdot 2 - 3 \cdot (-3)}
			</mrow>

			<mrow> \amp = -10 </mrow>

		</md>
		Substituting these results into our original cofactor expansion for <m>\det A</m>,
		we have
		<md>
			<mrow> \det A \amp = (-1) \cdot \det A_1 - 1 \cdot \det A_2 </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = (-1) \cdot 11 - 1 \cdot (-10) </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = -1 </mrow>
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
			1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 1
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Any row or column we choose will contain two zeros,
		so just expand along the first row:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A \amp =
				\begin{vmatrix}
					{\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^+ \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{0}}^- \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^+ \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{0}}^- \\
					            0             \amp             1             \amp             0             \amp             1             \\
					            1             \amp             0             \amp             1             \amp             0             \\
					            0             \amp             1             \amp             0             \amp             1
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				1 \cdot
				\begin{vmatrix}
					1 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
					1 \amp 0 \amp 1
				\end{vmatrix}
				+ 1 \cdot
				\begin{vmatrix}
					0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
					1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
					0 \amp 1 \amp 1
				\end{vmatrix}
			</mrow>

		</md>
		Let's compute these two <m>3 \times 3</m> minor determinants separately;
		call the first matrix <m>A_1</m> and the second <m>A_2</m>.
		The second row in each contains a couple of zeros,
		so we'll choose to expand along that row in each.
		In both expansions,
		we will use the general <m>a d - b c</m> formula for the resulting <m>2 \times 2</m> minor determinants
		(see <xref ref="subsection-det-concepts-2x2" />).
		<md>

			<mrow>
				\det A_1 \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{lll}
					            1             \amp             0             \amp             1             \\
					{\color{red}\mathbf{0}}^- \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^+ \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{0}}^- \\
					            1             \amp             0             \amp             1
				\end{avmatrix}

				\amp

				\det A_2 \amp =
				\begin{avmatrix}{lll}
					            0             \amp             1             \amp             1             \\
					{\color{red}\mathbf{1}}^- \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{0}}^+ \amp {\color{red}\mathbf{0}}^- \\
					            0             \amp             1             \amp             1
				\end{avmatrix}

			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp =
				1 \cdot
				\begin{vmatrix}
					1 \amp 1 \\
					1 \amp 1 \\
				\end{vmatrix}

				\amp

				\amp =
				-1 \cdot
				\begin{vmatrix}
					1 \amp 1 \\
					1 \amp 1
				\end{vmatrix}

			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				\amp = (1 \cdot 1 - 1 \cdot 1)
				\amp
				\amp = - (1 \cdot 1 - 1 \cdot 1)
			</mrow>

			<mrow> \amp = 0 \amp \amp = 0 </mrow>

		</md>
		Substituting these results into our original cofactor expansion for <m>\det A</m>,
		we have
		<md>
			<mrow> \det A \amp = 1 \cdot \det A_1 + 1 \cdot \det A_2 </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = 1 \cdot 0 + 1 \cdot 0 </mrow>
			<mrow> \amp = 0 </mrow>
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrrr}
			-4 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp 0 \\
			-1 \amp -1 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp 0 \\
			-6 \amp  4 \amp  2 \amp  0 \amp 0 \\
			 1 \amp -2 \amp  0 \amp  1 \amp 0 \\
			-5 \amp  6 \amp -2 \amp -4 \amp 2
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Notice that <m>A</m> is lower triangular,
		so by <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms-triangular">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms" /> its determinant is simply the product of its diagonal entries:
		<me>
			\det A
			= (-4) \cdot (-1) \cdot 2 \cdot 1 \cdot 2
			= 16
		</me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrrrrr}
			4 \amp 5 \amp  0 \amp  2 \amp -5 \amp -1 \\
			0 \amp 5 \amp -5 \amp  1 \amp -4 \amp -4 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \amp -2 \amp  0 \amp -3 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  4 \amp  1 \amp  3 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp -3 \amp -4 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  0 \amp  3
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Notice that <m>A</m> is upper triangular,
		so by <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms-triangular">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms" /> its determinant is simply the product of its diagonal entries:
		<me>
			\det A
			= 4 \cdot 5 \cdot (-1) \cdot 4 \cdot (-3) \cdot 3
			= 720
		</me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{abmatrix}{ccccccr}
			2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
			0 \amp 2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 3 \amp  0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -5
		\end{abmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Notice that <m>A</m> is diagonal,
		so by <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms-triangular">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms" /> its determinant is simply the product of its diagonal entries:
		<me>
			\det A
			= 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 4 \cdot 5 \cdot 4 \cdot 3 \cdot (-5)
			= -4800
		</me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m>\displaystyle
		\begin{bmatrix}
			5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp 0 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 5
		\end{bmatrix}
	</m></p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Let <m>A</m> represent the matrix.
		Notice that <m>A = 5 I</m> is scalar,
		so by <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms-scalar">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-det-special-forms" /> its determinant is simply the power of the common diagonal entry value,
		with exponent equal to the size of the square matrix:
		<me> \det A = 5^8 = 390625 </me>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
